#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f$. That is, there exists a function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\nabla f\n$$\n\nThe function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F$.\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f$, we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= \\frac{∂f}{∂x}i + \\frac{∂f}{∂y}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin (2x - y))i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sin (2x - y))j \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (2x - y)i - \\cos (2x - y)j \\\\\n&= F\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n2. $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n3. $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nbe a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> $\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D$.*\n\nNote that for each of our functions,\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F$, we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}$.\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G$, we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y$.\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$, we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H$, we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y$.\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "ae01904d-13f0-5d6e-ad3a-cc714c65765b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9500" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nbe a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> $\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D$.*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F$, the domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2$. Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2a}{1 + 9x^{2}} &= \\frac{3}{1 + 9x^{2}} \\\\\n2a &= 3 \\\\\na &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b69b16c0-aa13-5778-b2ef-bc583efe6b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y + 1)e^{xy}i + xe^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163305} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3d328d-a7a7-526c-bbd9-76e4717393d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + x^{2}e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163306} {:answer/id #uuid "c78bec72-c383-5d7e-b9a0-8f4e09c5580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xye^{xy}i + (x + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163307} {:answer/id #uuid "d55039d7-cd35-55e4-ad8f-f14b8bdfbbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + (xy + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163308} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f477f-887d-5565-aa72-281d9f2765d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ye^{xy}i + xye^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55039d7-cd35-55e4-ad8f-f14b8bdfbbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + (xy + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163308} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa74a3cb-2703-5e63-b05a-f75c6855f983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163304}] :question/id #uuid "ce34f68f-f91f-5032-a961-dafacf6d59a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104399" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y) = ye^{xy}$ a potential function?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc988d1e-536b-5f59-b865-472ba01e5c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163317} {:answer/id #uuid "beddba6f-6486-55fc-a77a-fc625e8de38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163318} {:answer/id #uuid "c402d630-70a8-5832-8705-ffb2cdc07958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163319} {:answer/id #uuid "26de321a-20a2-5291-8e5b-ea63b2b0159e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163320} {:answer/id #uuid "5cebda8d-5338-53eb-a058-413565f040a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cebda8d-5338-53eb-a058-413565f040a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163321} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97169ed-f827-5169-82cc-9a6ab6e2cc2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163316}] :question/id #uuid "ef2b9923-252b-570f-aaa0-557007eb97e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104570" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $F = xe^{xy}i + ye^{xy}j$\n2. $G = (xy + y^{2})i + (xy + x^{2})j$\n3. $H = \\cos (x - y)i - \\cos (x - y)j$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936079d6-0fe5-5c0f-a08d-88f69431508f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163587} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d53014-d488-5a50-8c39-a20bb22fa466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163588} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5779db-4033-5686-9e13-362d4aad1030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163589} {:answer/id #uuid "29a002fc-115e-5cd3-87ac-06e3814a53c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163590} {:answer/id #uuid "79f7aaa8-e34f-5045-a463-8a1caaa6fcc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79f7aaa8-e34f-5045-a463-8a1caaa6fcc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163591} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8679fe2-8fb6-50c9-9adb-edfdd7a1c59f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163586}] :question/id #uuid "86789f73-1843-5bdb-8f6f-64ce79172176" :question/math-academy-id "q-123996" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F = 2ayi + (3 - x)j$ on $R^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8b109e3-a408-5571-aa89-07dd23b07e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165092} {:answer/id #uuid "e105f644-d00e-5553-a513-a02693cf421f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165093} {:answer/id #uuid "ecebcb30-be67-5010-94b5-f4a07b861906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165094} {:answer/id #uuid "76029bdc-35d4-5686-a110-4a69ec373920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165095} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb5c98f-a6a6-5e4e-8c57-cba67a58ea9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb5c98f-a6a6-5e4e-8c57-cba67a58ea9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165096} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a86b01f-b6ec-53fc-8b0e-aad1d7fd42b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165091}] :question/id #uuid "321a61eb-7345-5f73-a2c6-72200c42218f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81745" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $F = (x^{2} + 2y)i + (x^{2} + 2y)j$\n2. $G = (ye^{x} + 2)i + (e^{x} + 4y)j$\n3. $H = yi + xj$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f81d2d-d5c5-56a4-9386-45b306a54421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi + x^{2}zj + (x^{2}y + 2z)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166387} {:answer/id #uuid "114c77c1-eeaa-5774-b9a8-841df8fb3176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}yzi + x^{2}yj - (2xy + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166388} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce24d24-00cc-5dfa-ae57-7b576ed54e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyzi - y^{2}zj + (y^{2}x + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166389} {:answer/id #uuid "3375422a-0420-5431-9e77-b5991e7f6f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi - x^{2}yj + (xz^{2} + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166390} {:answer/id #uuid "3658104d-2b4f-5a06-af82-d41092238392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyzi - x^{2}j + 4z^{2}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88f81d2d-d5c5-56a4-9386-45b306a54421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi + x^{2}zj + (x^{2}y + 2z)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166387} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ee6962-d399-5885-8c69-5ff1918c56ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166386}] :question/id #uuid "9e4d7c82-2e83-562b-b6bc-3ec6c276e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98947" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}yz + z^{2}$ a potential function?"}]}
